  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Mercantour is one of the natural gems of the Southern Alps — but it is also a national park, with a rule that has no exception for visitors: dogs are strictly banned from the park's core zone, even on a leash or carried. The park explains that this ban has existed since it was created in 1979, that it appears in the ministerial decrees of all national parks, and that it also applies to residents of the protected zone, except by special permission.</p>

      <h2>Good to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The rules for the core zone are set by the French Environment Code and the decree of 29 April 2009, detailed in the park's charter (decree of 28 December 2012). The only dogs allowed are those used to protect, drive and guard flocks, dogs guiding blind people or assisting disabled people and, under conditions, those of residents of hamlets reachable by road, kept tied up or in an enclosure near the buildings. The park's FAQ also mentions rescue and police dogs.</p>
      <p>Why this rule? The park cites the parasites dogs pass on to wild hoofed animals (such as the "tournis" disease of chamois and ibex), their hunting instinct even in play, the ground nests of black grouse, ptarmigan and rock partridge, the litters of marmots and hares, and conflicts with flocks and their guardian dogs. On the ground, the core zone is marked by green hexagons.</p>
      <p>To hike with your dog, the park invites you to choose a route outside the core, in the buffer zone (aire d'adhésion), where the decree sets no environmental rules specific to the park. Each municipality's rules still need checking, and keep your dog on a leash as soon as a flock is nearby. The park offers routes outside the core on its rando.marittimemercantour.eu platform, and the zone map is available on its website: check that your route stays in the buffer zone before setting off.</p>
      <p>Around Allos, in the Alpes-de-Haute-Provence, a tool called Pastorando helps you locate flocks, and therefore the guardian dogs (patous) that come with them. The Alpes Provence Verdon community of municipalities launched it in 2022 in Allos, then extended it in 2023 to Villars-Colmars and Les Lignins (Colmars-les-Alpes), and in 2024 to summer pastures in Thorame-Haute and La Mure-Argens. Flocks are located every hour by sensors, as a guide only; one aim of the tool is to avoid bringing your pet if a flock is on your planned route, but it does not cover the whole Mercantour.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: head for the valleys and trails in the buffer zone, just as spectacular and far more numerous than the park's core zone, which covers only a tiny part of France.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The resorts and villages around the Mercantour (Tinée, Vésubie and Ubaye valleys) offer many hiking starting points in the buffer zone, to explore with your dog on a leash, making sure your route stays outside the park's core zone.</p>
